BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261117T210000Z
DTEND:20261117T221000Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:21JPSI Speaker Series "The U.S.' Shifting Asia Strategy: Implicat
 ions for U.S. Allies" (Dr. Zack Cooper (AEI/Princeton Univ)))
DESCRIPTION:This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\,
  and members of the community.Co-sponsored by the Institute for Korean St
 udies and Department of Political Science. Please join us!  ————�
 ��———————–About the talk: Fifteen years ago\, the United 
 States promised to "pivot" to Asia. But today Washington continues to be 
 distracted by events in the Middle East and beyond. As Washington adjusts
  its approach to Asia\, U.S. allies and partners are starting to rethink 
 their own strategies\, as are policymakers in Beijing. How will U.S. poli
 cy evolve in the years ahead\, and what implications will this have for J
 apan and other U.S. allies in Asia?About the speaker:Zack Cooper is a sen
 ior fellow at the American Enterprise Institute\, where he studies US str
 ategy in Asia\, including alliance dynamics and US-China competition. He 
 also teaches at Princeton University\, serves as chair of the board of th
 e Open Technology Fund\, and co-hosts the Net Assessment podcast.&#160\;B
 efore joining AEI\, Dr. Cooper was the senior fellow for Asian security a
 t the Center for Strategic and International Studies. He previously worke
 d as codirector of the Alliance for Securing Democracy and senior fellow 
 at the German Marshall Fund of the United States and research fellow at t
 he Center for Strategic and Budgetary Assessments. He also served on staf
 f at the National Security Council and in the Office of the Secretary of 
 Defense.  Dr. Cooper is the author of Tides of Fortune: The Rise and Decl
 ine of Great Militaries from Yale University Press. He has also co-author
 ed books\, reports\, and articles on US strategy and alliances in Asia. H
 is writing has appeared in academic journals and popular press\, includin
 g International Security\,Security Studies\, Foreign Affairs\, the New Yo
 rk Times\, the Washington Post\, and the Wall Street Journal\, among othe
 r outlets. Dr. Cooper graduated from Princeton University with a PhD and 
 an MA in security studies and an MPA in international relations. He recei
 ved a BA in public policy from Stanford University.Prof. Adam LIFF (host/
 moderator) is Professor of East Asian International Relations at Indiana 
 University's Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies\, 
 where he also serves as Founding Director of its 21st Century Japan Polit
 ics and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Se
 nior Fellow with the Center for Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings Inst
 itution\, as well as a Faculty Associate and Associate-in-Research at Har
 vard University's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Institut
 e of Japanese Studies.&#160\;His research examines contemporary internati
 onal security and foreign policy challenges in East Asia—with a particu
 lar focus on Japanese politics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan securi
 ty alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U
 .S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Pri
 nceton University\, a postgraduate research certificate in International 
 Politics from the University of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies 
 and Psychology from Stanford University&#160\;This event is made possible
  thanks to generous support from the Japan Foundation.All 21JPSI events a
 re 100% in-person unless otherwise noted.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>This public event is open to all stud
 ents\, faculty\, staff\, and members of the community.</em><em>Co-sponsor
 ed by the Institute for Korean Studies and Department of Political Scienc
 e. Please join us!</em></p><p>  ————————————–</
 p><p dir="ltr"><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><strong><span sty
 le="text-decoration: underline\;">About the talk:</span></strong> Fifteen
  years ago\, the United States promised to "pivot" to Asia. But today Was
 hington continues to be distracted by events in the Middle East and beyon
 d.</span> As Washington adjusts its approach to Asia\, U.S. allies and pa
 rtners are starting to rethink their own strategies\, as are policymakers
  in Beijing. How will U.S. policy evolve in the years ahead\, and what im
 plications will this have for Japan and other U.S. allies in Asia?</p><p>
 <strong><span style="text-decoration: underline\;">About the speaker</spa
 n>:</strong><strong>Zack Cooper</strong> is a senior fellow at the Americ
 an Enterprise Institute\, where he studies US strategy in Asia\, includin
 g alliance dynamics and US-China competition. He also teaches at Princeto
 n University\, serves as chair of the board of the Open Technology Fund\,
  and co-hosts the Net Assessment podcast.&#160\;Before joining AEI\, Dr. 
 Cooper was the senior fellow for Asian security at the Center for Strateg
 ic and International Studies. He previously worked as codirector of the A
 lliance for Securing Democracy and senior fellow at the German Marshall F
 und of the United States and research fellow at the Center for Strategic 
 and Budgetary Assessments. He also served on staff at the National Securi
 ty Council and in the Office of the Secretary of Defense.</p><p>  Dr. Coo
 per is the author of <em>Tides of Fortune: The Rise and Decline of Great 
 Militaries</em> from Yale University Press. He has also co-authored books
 \, reports\, and articles on US strategy and alliances in Asia. His writi
 ng has appeared in academic journals and popular press\, including <em>In
 ternational Security\,</em><em>Security Studies</em>\, <em>Foreign Affair
 s</em>\, the <em>New York Times</em>\, the <em>Washington Post</em>\, and
  the <em>Wall Street Journal</em>\, among other outlets. Dr. Cooper gradu
 ated from Princeton University with a PhD and an MA in security studies a
 nd an MPA in international relations. He received a BA in public policy f
 rom Stanford University.</p><p><strong>Prof. Adam LIFF (host/moderator)</
 strong> is Professor of East Asian International Relations at Indiana Uni
 versity's Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies\, whe
 re he also serves as Founding Director of its 21st Century Japan Politics
  and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senio
 r Fellow with the Center for Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings Institu
 tion\, as well as a Faculty Associate and Associate-in-Research at Harvar
 d University's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Institute o
 f Japanese Studies.&#160\;His research examines contemporary internationa
 l security and foreign policy challenges in East Asia—with a particular
  focus on Japanese politics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan security 
 alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.
 -Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Prince
 ton University\, a postgraduate research certificate in International Pol
 itics from the University of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies and
  Psychology from Stanford University&#160\;</p><p><em>This event is made 
 possible thanks to generous support from the Japan Foundation.</em></p><p
 ><em>All 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unless otherwise noted.</em></p
 >
