BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261204T200000Z
DTEND:20261204T210000Z
LOCATION:Geography Building
SUMMARY:Rethinking Refugee Integration: Evidence from Newly Resettled Afg
 hans in the United States
DESCRIPTION:U.S. refugee policy continues to emphasize rapid employment a
 nd economic self-sufficiency as primary indicators of successful integrat
 ion. Drawing on a program of research with newly resettled Afghan househo
 lds in Missouri and Oregon\, this presentation examines refugee integrati
 on through a multidimensional lens that extends beyond employment and inc
 ome.  Using survey data from Afghan households in St. Louis\, Missouri (N
  = 184) and Portland\, Oregon (N = 150)\, alongside a national sample of 
 refugee households from the Annual Survey of Refugees (N = 1\,386)\, we e
 xamine multidimensional deprivation and its determinants among refugees r
 esettled in the United States. Findings reveal widespread multidimensiona
 l deprivation\, affecting 62% of households in St. Louis\, 74% in Portlan
 d\, and 70% of the national refugee sample. Household size and English la
 nguage proficiency emerged as consistent predictors of deprivation across
  settings. At the same time\, local contexts mattered: lower housing cost
 s and greater economic security in St. Louis were associated with lower r
 ates of multidimensional deprivation compared with Portland.  To better u
 nderstand how refugees experience integration beyond material conditions\
 , we draw on a follow-up mixed-methods study of 203 Afghan households and
  five focus group discussions. Quantitative findings indicate that strong
 er social connectedness within Afghan communities was associated with hig
 her quality of life\, whereas integration with Americans was not. Qualita
 tive findings revealed strong intra-ethnic social ties alongside limited 
 bridging connections to the broader host society. Participants described 
 declining quality of life during the first five years of resettlement des
 pite a strong sense of community belonging.  Together\, these findings ch
 allenge employment-centered approaches to refugee integration and highlig
 ht the importance of multidimensional frameworks that account for materia
 l well-being\, social connectedness\, and quality of life.  All Colloquiu
 m events will be held in Student Building Room 005 from 3:00 PM- 4:00 PM 
 and on Zoom at: https://iu.zoom.us/j/81721952791?jst=1
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">  U.
 S. refugee policy continues to emphasize rapid employment and economic se
 lf-sufficiency as primary indicators of successful integration. Drawing o
 n a program of research with newly resettled Afghan households in Missour
 i and Oregon\, this presentation examines refugee integration through a m
 ultidimensional lens that extends beyond employment and income.</div><div
 ></div><div>  Using survey data from Afghan households in St. Louis\, Mis
 souri (N = 184) and Portland\, Oregon (N = 150)\, alongside a national sa
 mple of refugee households from the Annual Survey of Refugees (N = 1\,386
 )\, we examine multidimensional deprivation and its determinants among re
 fugees resettled in the United States. Findings reveal widespread multidi
 mensional deprivation\, affecting 62% of households in St. Louis\, 74% in
  Portland\, and 70% of the national refugee sample. Household size and En
 glish language proficiency emerged as consistent predictors of deprivatio
 n across settings. At the same time\, local contexts mattered: lower hous
 ing costs and greater economic security in St. Louis were associated with
  lower rates of multidimensional deprivation compared with Portland.</div
 ><div></div><div>  To better understand how refugees experience integrati
 on beyond material conditions\, we draw on a follow-up mixed-methods stud
 y of 203 Afghan households and five focus group discussions. Quantitative
  findings indicate that stronger social connectedness within Afghan commu
 nities was associated with higher quality of life\, whereas integration w
 ith Americans was not. Qualitative findings revealed strong intra-ethnic 
 social ties alongside limited bridging connections to the broader host so
 ciety. Participants described declining quality of life during the first 
 five years of resettlement despite a strong sense of community belonging.
 </div><div></div><div>  Together\, these findings challenge employment-ce
 ntered approaches to refugee integration and highlight the importance of 
 multidimensional frameworks that account for material well-being\, social
  connectedness\, and quality of life.</div><div></div><div>  All Colloqui
 um events will be held in Student Building Room 005 from 3:00 PM- 4:00 PM
  and on Zoom at: https://iu.zoom.us/j/81721952791?jst=1</div>
